@page "/filter"

<PageTitle>Filter · Bortable</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Filter</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        The <code>Filter</code> predicate marks items that must not be dragged. Try dragging the red item - it can't be
        picked up. The other items can still be dragged past it, which shifts it along by one.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A locked item</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>Filter</code> is asked about every item: <code>true</code> locks it. A locked item gets the
        <code>bit-bortable-filtered</code> class from the list - which this page paints red - and cannot be picked up by
        the mouse, a finger or the keyboard.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <Bortable @bind-Items="_items" TItem="string" Id="filter-list" Filter="IsLocked"
                  Class="demo-list" ItemClass="demo-item" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
            <ItemTemplate Context="item">@item</ItemTemplate>
        </Bortable>

        <p class="demo-order"><strong>Order:</strong> <span id="filter-order">@string.Join(", ", _items)</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Locks that change</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The predicate reads your data, so a lock follows it: each task here has a lock button, and a locked task stays
        put until it is unlocked. The button sits inside the item, and a click on it is the button's - it never starts a
        drag.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <Bortable @bind-Items="_tasks" TItem="TaskItem" Id="filter-tasks" AriaLabel="Tasks"
                  Filter="t => t.Locked" ItemLabel="t => t.Title"
                  Class="demo-list" ItemClass="demo-item" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
            <ItemTemplate Context="task">
                <span class="demo-row">
                    <span class="demo-row__text">@task.Title</span>
                    <button type="button" class="demo-icon-btn" aria-pressed="@(task.Locked ? "true" : "false")"
                            aria-label="@($"Lock {task.Title}")" @onclick="() => task.Locked = !task.Locked">@(task.Locked ? "🔒" : "🔓")</button>
                </span>
            </ItemTemplate>
        </Bortable>

        <p class="demo-order"><strong>Tasks:</strong> <span id="filter-tasks-order">@string.Join(", ", _tasks.Select(t => t.Locked ? $"{t.Title} (locked)" : t.Title))</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<DemoCode />

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Try it</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Try to drag <em>Filtered (locked)</em>: nothing happens, and the cursor says it is not allowed.</li>
        <li>
            Drag <em>Item 3</em> below the locked item: the locked item moves up a place. A filter keeps an item from being
            picked up; it does not pin it to its index, as in SortableJS.
        </li>
        <li>
            From the keyboard, focus the locked item and press <kbd>Space</kbd>: a screen reader hears "Filtered (locked)
            cannot be moved." - the <code>Locked</code> message.
        </li>
        <li>Lock <em>Deploy</em>, try to drag it, unlock it, and drag it again.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Good to know</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            <code>Filter</code> is a predicate on the item, not a CSS selector as in SortableJS: it is asked with your own
            typed item, so the rule lives next to your data.
        </li>
        <li>
            It locks an item against the user's drags only. A move made by code - <code>MoveAsync</code>,
            <code>MoveToAsync</code> - still moves it, and in a <a href="multi-drag">multi-drag</a> list a locked item
            cannot be selected.
        </li>
        <li>
            The stylesheet dims a filtered item (<code>--bit-bortable-filtered-opacity</code>, <code>0.7</code>) and gives
            it the not-allowed cursor; restyle <code>bit-bortable-filtered</code> to make it your own.
        </li>
        <li>To lock a whole list instead, see <code>Disabled</code> on <a href="disable-sort">disabling sorting</a>.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    private IList<string> _items = new List<string>
    {
        "Item 1", "Item 2", "Item 3", "Filtered (locked)", "Item 4", "Item 5"
    };

    private IList<TaskItem> _tasks = new List<TaskItem>
    {
        new() { Title = "Write the tests" },
        new() { Title = "Release notes", Locked = true },
        new() { Title = "Deploy" },
        new() { Title = "Announce" },
    };

    private bool IsLocked(string item) => item.StartsWith("Filtered", StringComparison.Ordinal);

    public sealed class TaskItem
    {
        public required string Title { get; init; }
        public bool Locked { get; set; }
    }
}
